WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.760
This episode is sponsored by Wren, a website&nbsp;
where you calculate your carbon footprint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.400
And you can also sign up to make a monthly&nbsp;
contribution to offset your carbon footprint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.920
or support rainforest protection projects.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:31.840
When you are in the world of microbes, it does not&nbsp;
seem necessary to think of the largest land mammal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:38.800
on earth. What could elephants possibly have to&nbsp;
do with a ciliate? When you say this out loud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:46.080
any potential connection seems dubious at best.
However, if you look at the Dileptus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.720
you see an elongated, moving body, one that&nbsp;
seems like it’s made up entirely of snout—like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:58.160
an elephant’s trunk, disembodied and shrunk&nbsp;
down so that it can survive all on its own.&nbsp;

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:04.800
But unlike the elephant, Dileptus is no gentle&nbsp;
giant. It isn’t moving around because it wants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:11.280
to play or drink water. No,the dileptus is&nbsp;
waving around because it is loaded with toxins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:16.080
and it wants to put them to good use.
Now, this may be surprising given its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.360
general blob-like appearance, but the dileptus’&nbsp;
body is divided into two distinct regions:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.720
the proboscis and the trunk, which is confusing&nbsp;
because the trunk on the dileptus is not actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:33.120
the elephant trunk-like part of the organism.
The trunk is, in this case, more like the trunk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.600
of a tree, it’s the base for the rest of the&nbsp;
dileptus’ body. The proboscis is the snout, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:44.800
part that extends to the rest of the microcosmos&nbsp;
and examines it, looking for something to eat.&nbsp;

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:52.080
And interestingly where the proboscis and&nbsp;
the trunk connect lies the dileptus’ mouth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:58.000
a cavity lined with hair-like cilia that continues&nbsp;
down the length of the proboscis. The dileptus can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.600
use these cilia to generate currents in the&nbsp;
water that carry food to its oral cavity. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.360
can imagine it a bit like if your arm hairs could&nbsp;
stir up the air around you and levitate the snack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.400
in your hand all the way to your mouth. Which,&nbsp;
now that I’m thinking about it, sounds amazing.&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.160
But before the cilia can work their&nbsp;
magic, the dileptus has to catch its food.&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.600
And that’s unfortunate for the Spirostomum at&nbsp;
the bottom left corner. It does not know that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.560
on the other side of that piece of debris is&nbsp;
a predator that one scientists called “the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:37.440
king of beasts among the ciliated protozoa”&nbsp;
with an appetite that is quote “insatiable.”&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:44.800
The dileptus is not a subtle, lying in wait kind&nbsp;
of predator. It’s always moving, always seeking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.240
And when its proboscis makes&nbsp;
contact with the spirostomum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:54.960
it grabs a chunk of its body almost instantly.
The spirostomum is large and nimble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:01.040
and it pulls away quickly as if it’s been stung.&nbsp;
But it leaves behind a tasty morsel of itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:17.840
which the DIleptus draws in until it’s&nbsp;
close enough to open wide and eat.&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:28.800
And from its safe distance away, the spirostomum&nbsp;
may not be whole again yet, but it is at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:34.720
out of reach of the dileptus, and of its&nbsp;
proboscis, and its hundreds of toxicysts.&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.120
You can actually see those toxicysts&nbsp;
here, when we look at the proboscis with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:45.680
1000x magnification. And while the proboscis’&nbsp;
movement through the water may seem like a chaotic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:52.000
mess of twisting left and right and up and down,&nbsp;
there is a surprising order to it all--driven by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:59.440
the placement of those toxic cellular weapons.
The dileptus has to move in a way that lets it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:05.680
immediately attack its prey upon contact, but&nbsp;
its proboscis is only lined with toxicysts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.760
on one side--the side that leads to the oral&nbsp;
cavity. So it always sweeps its proboscis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:18.240
with its toxicysts facing whatever is about to be&nbsp;
swept. And when it’s done moving in one direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:22.720
the dileptus rotates its proboscis&nbsp;
and sweeps in the other direction.&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.800
Again, this is all so that when the dileptus&nbsp;
finally hits something, it won’t just make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:33.040
contact: it will unload needles full of&nbsp;
toxins into whatever it has made contact with.&nbsp;

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:39.200
To learn a bit more about how these toxins work,&nbsp;
in one experiment, scientists filled a fluid with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:45.360
the material coming from the Dileptus’ toxins.&nbsp;
They then placed some Paramecium into the fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:49.600
to see how they would respond.
They did not respond well.&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:55.520
Immediately, the paramecium tried to mount their&nbsp;
own defenses by releasing their own harpooning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.240
trichocysts. Now, that might have worked&nbsp;
against an actual dileptus, pitting ciliate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.360
against ciliate as they poke each other for&nbsp;
survival. But in this experiment, with the toxins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.840
permeating the fluid, the best the paramecium&nbsp;
could hope for was to delay the inevitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:17.840
to use its trichocysts as a temporary fence&nbsp;
until eventually, it emerged to its own death.&nbsp;

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:24.000
Now, that is an extreme situation, one that&nbsp;
a paramecium would probably not actually find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.040
itself in were it anywhere else but in a lab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.800
Fortunately for paramecium, we do not live in a&nbsp;
world full of Dileptus toxin fluid. Those toxins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.840
are kept safely packaged within the organism&nbsp;
themselves, waiting for their designated time.&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.000
Which does lead us to a final question—one&nbsp;
that we do not have an answer for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.920
but that we like thinking about nonetheless.
We’ve seen the dileptus as a solitary organism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:56.960
and we’ve also seen it in groups with others of&nbsp;
its kind. But how? How does an organism that is so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:03.920
simple, so reactive, so toxic—how is it able&nbsp;
to just casually entwine itself with others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:09.440
of its own kind? How can they gather without&nbsp;
immediately all jabbing each other to death?&nbsp;

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:16.800
Well, we don’t know, but it means that there must&nbsp;
be more to the release of toxicysts than just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:22.160
simple mechanical stimulus of touch—that there&nbsp;
must be some sort of recognition that makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.600
possible for two Dileptuses to know each&nbsp;
other. You might even call it chemistry.&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.360
Now, we don’t know the underlying signs and&nbsp;
signals that make that recognition possible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:38.960
But what drove this mutually assured safety&nbsp;
may have actually been something selfish.&nbsp;

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:42.960
Imagine for a moment that the palm of&nbsp;
your hand was covered in toxic needles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:49.280
and every time you touched your own body, your&nbsp;
palm tore off a piece of it. You would probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:53.920
not last long as an organism. You would need&nbsp;
some kind of way to tell your palm that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:59.520
thing it is touching is in fact the body&nbsp;
it belongs to and to not try and damage it.&nbsp;

00:06:59.520 --> 00:07:04.160
The same may have been true for the ancestors&nbsp;
of Dileptus and of other organisms that have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:11.200
toxicysts. They may have kept accidentally hurting&nbsp;
themselves over and over, often beyond repair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:17.120
until evolution provided a new path, one where&nbsp;
they would be able to recognize themselves—and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.560
perhaps eventually, even others of their own kind.&nbsp;

